    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Anybuddy is a learning toy that aims to help kids ages 6-9 practice social skills related to theory of mind by allowing them to turn almost anything in their world into a talking, listening character. By programming the toy to carry a conversation with another person, kids are required to make decisions about what Anybuddy should say, when it should listen, and what it should listen for. Anybuddy consists of a set of separate facial features that can be attached to magnetic objects to create various emotional expressions such as happy, sad, mad, or scared. The logic of Anybuddy’s conversations is programmed by kids using a companion app that utilizes a visual, block-based programming language. Anybuddy was designed to work using automatic speech recognition technology. Usability testing was conducted with a prototype of this design, and through in-home observations. Expected benefits of this learning technology were an increased understanding and competency in developmentally-appropriate social skills, particularly perspective-taking.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
